From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_bcm: honor max-speed on ACPI-enumerated devices

The ACPI resource walk hardcodes oper_speed = 4000000 when it sees the
UART serial bus resource.  Devicetree platforms describe the rate the
board actually supports through the "max-speed" property, and
bcm_of_probe() reads it - but a board enumerated via ACPI (PRP0001 or
otherwise) with a "max-speed" _DSD property had it silently ignored,
and setup tried 4 MBaud against hardware validated for less.

Read the property after the resource walk so it overrides the default,
exactly as the DT path does.  Boards without the property keep the
4000000 default.

Validated on a Khadas VIM3 (BCM4359 on uart_A, PRP0001 serdev child):
without this the vendor clock/baud commands fail and setup falls back
to 115200; with it the link runs at the described 2 MBaud with
hardware flow control.
---
diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
index 9a103db7e355..d60bdc1e66aa 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
@@ -1207,6 +1207,13 @@ static int bcm_acpi_probe(struct bcm_device *dev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The UART resource walk above defaults oper_speed to 4000000.
+	 * Boards enumerated via PRP0001 describe the supported rate the
+	 * same way devicetree does - honor it, as bcm_of_probe() would.
+	 */
+	device_property_read_u32(dev->dev, "max-speed", &dev->oper_speed);
+
 	if (irq_polarity != -1) {
 		dev->irq_active_low = irq_polarity;
 		dev_warn(dev->dev, "Overwriting IRQ polarity to active %s by module-param\n",
